{
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "route": {
    "route_id": "jaffa-jerusalem",
    "name": "Jaffa-Jerusalem Railway",
    "name_arabic": "سكة حديد يافا-القدس",
    "countries": "Israel, Palestine",
    "gauge_mm": 1000,
    "year_opened": 1892,
    "era": "historic",
    "color": "#b45309",
    "corridor_confidence": "high",
    "description": "First railway in region; modern replacement 2018",
    "notes": "'First in region' holds for Palestine (Egypt’s 1854 line predates it region-wide). Modern line opened 2018.  [gauge_mm] Opened 1892 at 1,000 mm (metre) gauge; regauged to 1,050 mm (1915) then to 1,435 mm (standard) by 1920. The system’s 1,435 mm reflects the final/modern gauge, not the original.  [route] CORRECTIONS: the historic 1892 line ran up the SOREK valley (Jaffa-Lod-Ramla-Al-Sejed-Dayr Aban-Battir-Jerusalem). LATRUN and BAB AL-WAD from the project list were NOT stations on this railway — they lie on the Ayalon ROAD corridor (today’s Highway 1 / near the modern 2018 line), not the Sorek-valley alignment; held as off-line extra_stations. Added the missing Al-Sejed (km 39.5) and Dayr Aban (km 50.3).",
    "history": "The first railway in Palestine, opened 26 September 1892 at 1,000 mm gauge. Championed by the Ottoman Jewish entrepreneur Joseph Navon Bey and built by French interests, it ran ~86 km from Jaffa via Lod and Ramla, then climbed inland up the Wadi es-Surar (Nahal Sorek) valley via Battir to Jerusalem — a ~3.5-hour journey.",
    "verification_status": "unverified",
    "source_ids": [
      "cotterell_1984",
      "levantrain_system",
      "wp_israel_rw",
      "wp_jaffa_jerusalem"
    ]
  },
